    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Energy-Systems_JR-017448": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Energy Systems",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-14\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed term for 12 months Faculty: Engineering and Information Technology School: Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering   Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super  Join the Net Zero Australia Project & collaborate with Princeton and industry partners to shape clean energy infrastructure delivery Apply now to help deliver Australia's net zero transition through innovative, feasibility-focused energy and infrastructure research Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   Working closely with Dr Dominic Davis, you will help design realistic pathways to Australia's net zero future. You will develop energy system models that reflect real infrastructure development constraints across electricity, renewables and heavy industry. You will work closely with industry and government stakeholders, translating insights into practical policy and investment guidance. What you will deliver:   Design and implement energy system optimisation frameworks that incorporate staged, constrained infrastructure delivery sequences for Australia. Map and analyse project development pathways for clean energy and industrial decarbonisation assets, from pre-FID stages to operation. Conduct qualitative and quantitative research, including stakeholder interviews, to validate timelines, costs and decision bottlenecks. Identify and rank key transition delivery risks, producing clear recommendations for policy and investment decision-makers. Produce high-quality publications, reports and presentations, and contribute to the Department of Mechanical Engineering research culture. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed or near-completed PhD in engineering, economics, energy systems, finance or a related discipline. Demonstrate strong quantitative modelling experience, ideally in energy system optimisation, project finance or infrastructure analysis. Understand energy technologies and large-scale infrastructure delivery, including development stages and capital mobilisation processes. Communicate research clearly through writing and presentations, with a track record of high-quality outputs or reports. Collaborate effectively with academic, industry and government stakeholders, including conducting interviews and applied research engagements. Bring familiarity with energy markets, industrial decarbonisation, project scheduling tools or open-source energy modelling platforms. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team Joining the School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering, you will sit within Mechanical Engineering’s Thermodynamics Group. You will be part of the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology, a global leader in impact-driven research. The Net Zero Australia Project collaboration with Princeton University and the University of Queensland offers exceptional interdisciplinary networks and visibility. You will work closely with industry, government and community partners, informing real decisions on energy and infrastructure. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Position Description JR-017448 Research Fellow in Energy Systems PD.pdf JR-017448 Research Fellow in Energy Systems PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Indigenous-Graduate-Research-Program-Coordinator_JR-014225-1": {
      "title": "Indigenous Graduate Research Program Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-14\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosition Number:   JR-014225   Role type:   Part Time (0.8 FTE) ;   Continuing   Location:   Parkville   Faculty:   Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences (MDHS)     Department :   Melbourne Poche Centre for Indigenous Health   Salary:   Level   B   $124,656 -$148,023   plus 17% super   Pivotal Indigenous PhD   advancement   role / build culturally safe doctoral pathways / collaborate across a leading health faculty   Join Melbourne Poche Centre to lead Indigenous graduate research initiatives and   drive   Indigenous doctoral success in health.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic).        The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to   benefit   society through the transformative impact of education and research.   Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity     You will lead Indigenous graduate research initiatives that strengthen Indigenous doctoral success in health. You will design innovative programs, workshops and partnerships that support Indigenous PhD candidates across the faculty. You will contribute Indigenous scholarship and shape sector-wide conversations on Indigenous higher degree research.   What you will deliver:     Lead Indigenous graduate research strategy and   provide   high-level advice to the Director on Indigenous PhD initiatives.   Design and deliver masterclasses,   seminars   and bespoke academic support for Indigenous PhD students in MDHS.   Plan and deliver the biennial international Indigenous PhD workshop and other flagship graduate research events.   Build strong partnerships across faculties,   institutes   and external networks to enhance opportunities for Indigenous graduate researchers.   Secure funding and contribute research that strengthens Indigenous doctoral pathways and leadership in health.   You may be a great fit if you:     Identify as   an Aboriginal   and/ or Torres Strait Islander person .   Hold a PhD in a relevant field, or be near completion, with experience in Indigenous health or education.   Demonstrate experience designing and delivering academic programs that enhance opportunities for Indigenous graduate research students.   Bring deep understanding of Indigenous graduate researcher experiences and the broader higher education and research environment.   Excel at coordinating complex workloads, balancing competing   deadlines   and delivering outcomes in dynamic academic settings.   Build collaborative relationships across faculties,   institutes   and external partners, and contribute to inclusive Indigenous leadership.   For further   information   please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!     We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!     Your new team   The Melbourne Poche Centre for Indigenous Health accelerates Indigenous leadership in health across the MDHS faculty. The team works across schools and institutes, building pathways for Indigenous students,   researchers   and health leaders. You will join a small, committed Indigenous-focused team that values reciprocity,   relationality   and culturally safe practice. You will collaborate with leading health and medical partners locally,   nationally   and internationally to drive meaningful impact.   Be Yourself     The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe   organisation . The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.   Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're   committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.   Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants     We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit   our   Indigenous staff page   to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.     Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin   Korin , our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via   korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials:   Visit   \"apply with us\"   to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is   required   for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload a separate document addressing the Selection Criteria outlined in the Position Description.     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Prof   Shawana Andrews   via email at   shawanaa@unimelb.edu.au     For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply   today, and   join a community   that’s   shaping the future.   Applications close:   11 September 2026   at   11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:   PD_JR-014225 Indigenous Graduate Research Program Coordinator .docx PD_JR-014225 Indigenous Graduate Research Program Coordinator .pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Software-Engineer_JR-016385": {
      "title": "Research Software Engineer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 27 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full Time; Fixed term for 2 years Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry & Health Sciences Department : Department of Infectious Diseases Salary: Level A - $83,368 $113,862 OR Level B.1 $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super Work at a world-leading infectious diseases institute / build cutting-edge data platforms / support global pandemic therapeutics Join us to design robust data systems that accelerate research breakthroughs in infection and immunity. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will design and deliver data systems that power CGCPT’s pandemic therapeutics research. You will link complex biological and clinical datasets, enabling advanced AI and analytics across the Doherty Institute. You will collaborate with leading scientists and clinicians while growing your expertise in health data engineering. What you will deliver: Design and maintain databases and data-lakes that securely store and retrieve diverse biological and clinical data. Develop software infrastructure to link pathogen, immune response, clinical and epidemiological data at sample level. Implement data integration and sharing workflows that enable AI/ML training and analytics within and beyond CGCPT. Partner with governance, ethics, executive and research stakeholders to align data solutions with approvals and strategy. Contribute to grant proposals, presentations, seminars and supervision activities that strengthen data science capacity. You may be a great fit if you: Hold a Bachelor or postgraduate degree in Computer Science, Software Engineering, or an equivalent discipline. Bring proven experience delivering software engineering projects, from design through implementation and ongoing maintenance. Develop and optimise databases using SQL or similar technologies, including solutions for data integration. Use Git and GitHub confidently to manage code, version control, and collaborative development workflows. Build modern web applications with JavaScript or TypeScript, Node.js and contemporary web frameworks. Demonstrate strong self-management, stakeholder engagement and communication skills within multidisciplinary environments. For further information, please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – The Peter Doherty Institute is a world-leading centre for infectious diseases and immunity, with proven global impact. Within it, the Department of Infectious Diseases, CGCPT and Computational Sciences Initiative unite clinicians, scientists and data experts. Together, they harness cutting-edge computational and data sciences to transform pandemic preparedness and therapeutic discovery. Joining this team, you will collaborate across disciplines, access rich datasets, and drive meaningful health outcomes. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants  We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact David Price ( david.price1@unimelb.edu.au) and Lachlan Coin ( lachlan.coin@unimelb.edu.au ) For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 10 September. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-016385 Research Software Engineer PD.docx JR-016385 Research Software Engineer PD.pdf #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Clinical-Sites-Metro--Regional/Professor-of-Neonatology-and-Consultant-Neonatologist---Clinical-Academic-and-Research-Lead_JR-014381": {
      "title": "Professor of Neonatology and Consultant Neonatologist – Clinical Academic and Research Lead",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 28 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Full-time (0.5FTE Professor, 0.5FTE Clinical Specialist), Fixed term for 5 years Faculty: Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department /School:  Department of Obstetrics, Gynaecology and Newborn Health, Melbourne Medical School Clinical Partner:  Neonatal Services, The Royal Women’s Hospital Salary: Level E negotiable salary package p.a. plus employer superannuation contributions (17% University of Melbourne appointment; 12% Royal Women’s Hospital appointment)   Pivotal clinician-academic leadership role across the Women’s and the University of Melbourne / advance world-leading neonatal research, clinical excellence and education / collaborate across Melbourne’s leading Parkville Biomedical Precinct Apply now to shape the future of newborn health through research innovation, clinical leadership and national and international collaborations Investing in you – employer superannuation contributions (17% University of Melbourne appointment; 12% the Women’s appointment), benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. The Royal Women’s Hospital The Women’s is one of Australia’s oldest and most distinguished tertiary hospitals dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of women and newborns, offering specialised services within maternity, neonatal and women’s health. Read more about us . Your next career opportunity   The University of Melbourne and the Women’s invite applications from an internationally recognised clinician-academic leader to shape the future of newborn health through research excellence, clinical innovation and education.   This prestigious joint appointment brings together academic leadership within the University of Melbourne with strategic research leadership and specialist clinical expertise at the Women’s, one of Australia’s leading neonatal services. As Professor of Neonatology and Consultant Neonatologist – Clinical Academic and Research Lead, you will lead the growth of internationally recognised neonatal research, strengthen multidisciplinary partnerships and translate discovery into improved outcomes for newborns and families.   Located within Melbourne’s globally recognised Parkville Biomedical Precinct, this role provides an exceptional opportunity to collaborate across leading academic, clinical and research organisations to advance newborn health nationally and internationally.   What you will deliver:   Provide strategic leadership across neonatal research, clinical care and education. Lead internationally recognised research programs that address major challenges in newborn health. Strengthen research capability through collaboration, funding success and development of future clinician-scientists. Drive research translation to improve clinical practice and outcomes for newborns and families. Build partnerships across the University, the Women’s, research organisations and the broader health sector. Foster a culture of excellence, innovation and multidisciplinary collaboration.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a research doctorate, medical degree, Fellowship of the Royal Australasian College of Physicians (or equivalent) and specialist registration in Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine. Demonstrate an internationally recognised record of excellence in neonatal research, supported by an outstanding publication record and clinical impact. Have sustained success securing competitive, peer-reviewed research funding and building internationally recognised research programs. Deliver exceptional neonatal clinical care with a strong commitment to family-centred practice and evidence-based medicine. Lead diverse, multidisciplinary teams while fostering inclusive, psychologically safe environments that promote research excellence, education and innovation. Build strategic national and international collaborations with universities, hospitals, research institutes, professional bodies and funding organisations.   For further information please refer to the attached Position Description .  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment. For more information, check out our benefits pages: University of Melbourne benefits The Women’s benefits Advancing Newborn Health Through Partnership The University of Melbourne and the Women’s share a commitment to advancing the health and wellbeing of newborns and families through excellence in research, education, clinical care and innovation. Together, they form a world-leading clinical-academic partnership within Melbourne’s internationally recognised Parkville Biomedical Precinct, bringing together leading clinicians, researchers and health professionals to address complex challenges in newborn health. This collaborative environment provides an exceptional platform to advance internationally recognised neonatal research, translate discovery into clinical practice, strengthen multidisciplinary partnerships and deliver meaningful improvements in outcomes for newborns and their families.   Be Yourself   The Women’s and the University of Melbourne are Equal Opportunity Employers and child-safe organisations. The diversity of our communities enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb and at the Women’s .   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   Visit the University of Melbourne Indigenous staff page and the Women’s Aboriginal careers page to learn more about our investment in and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our University of Melbourne Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description . Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Sue Walker at spwalker@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Emily Haagensen at snr-talentacq@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Emily Haagensen on +61 428005853 or at snr-talentacq@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: Sunday 13 September 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:   JR-014381_Professor of Neonatology_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Clinical-Sites-Metro--Regional/Professor-of-Obstetrics-and-Gynaecology-and-Consultant-Obstetrician---Gynaecologist---Clinical-Academic-and-Research-Lead_JR-014383": {
      "title": "Professor of Obstetrics and Gynaecology and Consultant Obstetrician / Gynaecologist – Clinical Academic and Research Lead",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 28 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Full-time (0.5FTE Professor, 0.5FTE Clinical Specialist), Fixed term for 5 years Faculty: Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department /School:  Department of Obstetrics, Gynaecology and Newborn Health, Melbourne Medical School Clinical Partner: Maternity Services and Women’s Health, The Royal Women’s Hospital Salary: Level E negotiable salary package p.a. plus employer superannuation contributions (17% University of Melbourne appointment; 12% the Women’s appointment)   Pivotal clinician-academic leadership role across the Women’s and the University of Melbourne / advance world-leading research, clinical excellence and education in women’s health / collaborate across Melbourne’s leading Parkville Biomedical Precinct Apply now to shape the future of obstetrics and gynaecology through research innovation, clinical leadership and national and international collaborations across women’s health Investing in you – employer superannuation contributions (17% University of Melbourne appointment; 12% the Women’s appointment), benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   The Royal Women’s Hospital The Women’s is one of Australia’s oldest and most distinguished tertiary hospitals dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of women and newborns, offering specialised services within maternity, neonatal and women’s health. Read more about us . Your next career opportunity   The University of Melbourne and the Women's invite applications from internationally recognised clinician-academic leaders to join one of Australia's leading academic health partnerships. This prestigious joint appointment combines academic leadership within the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences with strategic research leadership and specialist clinical practice at the Women's.   As Professor of Obstetrics and Gynaecology and Consultant Obstetrician/Gynaecologist – Clinical Academic and Research Lead, you will provide visionary leadership across research, clinical care and education to advance excellence in women's health. You will build internationally competitive research programs, strengthen multidisciplinary collaborations, mentor future clinician-scientists and translate research discoveries into meaningful improvements in clinical practice, policy and health outcomes.   Located within Melbourne's internationally renowned Parkville Biomedical Precinct, this role offers an exceptional opportunity to collaborate across leading universities, hospitals and research institutes while shaping the future of women's health nationally and globally.   What you will deliver:   Provide strategic leadership across research, clinical care and education. Build internationally recognised research capability and multidisciplinary collaborations. Drive research translation to improve outcomes for women, babies and families. Mentor and develop the next generation of clinician-scientists and academic leaders. Strengthen partnerships across the University, the Royal Women's Hospital and the broader research community. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a medical qualification registrable with the Medical Board of Australia and Fellowship of the Royal Australian and New Zealand College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (or equivalent). Demonstrate an internationally recognised record of excellence in women's health, obstetrics and gynaecology research. Have an outstanding track record of research leadership, competitive grant success and developing high-impact research programs. Are an experienced clinician with a commitment to excellence in specialist obstetric and gynaecological care. Have successfully led multidisciplinary teams and built productive collaborations across academic, clinical and research environments. Are passionate about developing future clinician-scientists, mentoring emerging researchers and advancing excellence in education and research translation.   For further information please refer to the attached Position Description .  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment. For more information, check out our benefits pages: University of Melbourne benefits The Women’s benefits Advancing Women's Health Through Partnership The University of Melbourne and the Women's share a commitment to advancing the health and wellbeing of women, babies and families through excellence in research, education, clinical care and innovation. Together, they form a world-leading clinical-academic partnership within Melbourne's internationally recognised Parkville Biomedical Precinct, bringing together leading clinicians, researchers and educators to address complex challenges in women's health. This collaborative environment provides an exceptional platform to drive internationally recognised research, translate discovery into practice, strengthen multidisciplinary partnerships and deliver meaningful impact for communities in Australia and around the world.   Be Yourself   The Women’s and the University of Melbourne are Equal Opportunity Employers and child-safe organisations. The diversity of our communities enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb and at the Women’s .   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   Visit the University of Melbourne Indigenous staff page and the Women’s Aboriginal careers page to learn more about our investment in and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our University of Melbourne Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description . Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Sue Walker at spwalker@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Emily Haagensen at snr-talentacq@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Emily Haagensen on +61 428005853 or at snr-talentacq@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: Sunday 13 September 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-014383_Professor of Obstetrics_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Assistant-in-Computational-Drug-Discovery_JR-016771": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Computational Drug Discovery",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 28 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full Time   (1.0 FTE) ; Fixed term for 2 years     Faculty:  Faculty of Science    School:   School of   BioSciences     Salary:  Level A :   $87,266 - $118,416   p.a.   plus 17% super     Advance computational approaches to contraceptive discovery   / Shape global reproductive health resources and tools / Grow your research career at   the Bio21 Institute   Join us to drive high-impact computational biology, develop innovative 'omics pipelines and publish   high-impact   reproductive health research.     Investing in   you   – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne     We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to  benefit  society through the transformative impact of education and research.  Discover more via our website.     Your next career opportunity       In this role, you will lead computational analyses for a collaborative contraceptive discovery  programme . You will design robust 'omics pipelines, develop public resources and co- author  high-impact publications and tools. You will also gain mentoring, collaboration opportunities and visibility across the School of  BioSciences  and partners.     What you will deliver:       Lead analysis of large-scale 'omics datasets, including single-cell, spatial transcriptomics,  proteomics  and metabolomics, for contraceptive discovery.     Develop,  document  and  maintain  reproducible, version-controlled  analysis  pipelines in R and/or Python on high-performance computing infrastructure.     Prepare figures, reports,  manuscripts  and   online   applications that communicate complex analyses to collaborators and the wider community.     Contribute to  research funding proposals, data sharing, and upload of curated datasets to  appropriate public  repositories.     Support training and supervision of students and junior  researchers, and  participate in School and Faculty engagement activities.     You may be a great fit if you:       Hold a PhD in a relevant computational discipline, or a  Masters  plus substantial computational biology experience.     Bring strong experience  analysing  sequencing data, ideally including single-cell and bulk RNA-seq datasets.     Demonstrate advanced R and/or Python skills, using tools such as Seurat,  Scanpy , DESeq2 or  edgeR .     Apply modern machine learning and statistical modelling methods to high-dimensional data with appropriate experimental design and control.     Use version control and workflow tools to build transparent, well-documented and reproducible  analysis  pipelines across projects.     Show evidence of research productivity and a clear desire to build an academic career in reproductive health.     For further information, please refer to the attached PD.       What we offer you!       We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:      Build future financial security with 17% superannuation     Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being      Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning     Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave     Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company      Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    ​   For more information check out our benefits page!      Your new team     You will join the School of  BioSciences , a vibrant community tackling global biological challenges from reproductive health to climate change. Working within the Faculty of Science, you will collaborate with experts in genomics, data  science  and molecular biology. The  School  offers outstanding facilities, diverse  partnerships  and a supportive culture that values curiosity,  inclusion  and career development.     Be Yourself       The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe  organisation . The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.  Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at   UniMelb .     We're  committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.  Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.     Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants       We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit  our   Indigenous staff page  to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.      Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin  Korin , our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via   korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au     Application essentials:     Visit  \"apply with us\"  to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.     This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is  required  for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.      Please upload your  resume and a cover letter  outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.     Please upload a  separate document addressing the Selection Criteria  outlined in the Position Description.         Want to know more?     For queries related to this specific position, contact Brittany Goods via email at  britt.goods@unimelb.edu.au       For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at  hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au     For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at  hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au  (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').      Apply  today, and  join a community  that’s  shaping the future.     Applications close : 11 September   2026   at  11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.      Position Description:     PD_JR-016771 Research Assistant in Computational Drug Discovery.docx PD_JR-016771 Research Assistant in Computational Drug Discovery.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer---Senior-Lecturer-in-Mathematics-Education_JR-008985-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Mathematics Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-12\nPosted: Posted 28 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Continuing    Faculty: Science Department / School:  School of Mathematics and Statistics   Salary: Level of appointment is subject to qualification and experience  Level B: AUD $124,656 - $148,023  p.a.   Level C: AUD $​152,695 - $176,065 p.a.   plus 17% super  (retirement savings system/pension scheme)   Expand mathematics and statistics education expertise / Lead MSLC teaching innovation and support / Achieve national and international impact Apply now to contribute nationally and internationally to maths education excellence.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   The School of Mathematics and Statistics is seeking to expand its expertise in mathematics and/or statistics education, with an appointment at Level B or C depending on your experience.   You will contribute to and lead an innovative academic program of teaching, along with research in mathematics education.   You also will contribute to the national and international community through the conduct of research in mathematics education, or scholarship of learning and teaching in mathematics. There may also be opportunities for the candidate to conduct other discipline-related research.  What you will deliver:   Design and deliver engaging first- and second-year undergraduate subjects, including lectures, tutorials and computer laboratory classes. Coordinate large undergraduate subjects, ensuring high-quality curricula, assessments, student support and effective use of digital platforms. Lead school-level innovation in mathematics and statistics education, contributing to curriculum development and improved teaching practices. Provide academic leadership within the MSLC, overseeing tutor recruitment, training, workload allocation and assessment administration. Develop a coherent research or scholarship programme in mathematics education, sharing outcomes through publications, presentations and practice. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in mathematics, statistics, or another closely related discipline. Demonstrate excellence in undergraduate mathematics or statistics teaching and curriculum development across varied class sizes. Bring evidence of growing national or international recognition in mathematics education or scholarship of learning and teaching. Lead or coordinate large undergraduate subjects and drive educational innovation in mathematics or statistics. Apply strong organisational and digital literacy skills to manage deadlines, systems, and educational technologies effectively. Understand the transition challenges faced by first-year students and evolving post-pandemic mathematics education landscapes. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – “School of Mathematics and Statistics”  The University of Melbourne's School of Mathematics and Statistics is one of Australia's leading mathematics and statistics schools. It has achieved this status through the high quality of its research and teaching programs. The School offers a wide range of subjects to undergraduate and postgraduate students and is involved in aspects of community life that impact on the interests of the School and the discipline.  The School of Mathematics and Statistics has a total of 90 continuing teaching and/or research staff; 37 research only staff and consultants; 19 academic specialists and 16 support staff. In 2023, there were 143 Research Higher Degree and 189 Coursework Master of Science students. The School has had 10 staff members inducted as Fellows of the Academy of Science.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, or related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 11 September 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:     JR-008985 Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Mathematics Education_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Horizon-EU-Specialist_JR-015137-2": {
      "title": "Horizon EU Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 29 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:   Full time; Fixed term until December 2027   Department / School:    Melbourne Research and Enterprise   Salary:   $125,191 - $135,504   p.a. plus 17% super    Strategic senior specialist role shaping EU funding engagement / drive Horizon Europe success across the University / global research impact   Join us to lead Horizon Europe readiness, empowering researchers to secure large-scale international funding.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne     We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to   benefit   society through the transformative impact of education and research.   Discover more via our website.   Australia joining Horizon Europe    The Australian Government has successfully concluded negotiations for association to Horizon Europe Pillar II. This will allow Australian researchers to join international and cross-sectoral R&D consortia through the final year of a AU$155 billion funding pool.   Horizon Europe Pillar II opens opportunities for Australian researchers to access the largest multilateral research program in the world, spanning six thematic clusters.   Seizing the global potential of networks through Horizon Europe offers a clear opportunity to accelerate our international research collaborations, not just with Europe but with other associate d   countries around the world and our regional neighbours.   Australia and the University are well positioned to drive impactful research through Horizon Europe, by activating a strong network of existing trusted partnerships and collaborations.   Your next career opportunity     You will play a pivotal role in positioning Melbourne as a leading participant in Horizon Europe Pillar II.   You will translate complex European funding frameworks into clear strategies, capability   programs   and targeted support for researchers   and support staff spanning the whole grant life cycle .   You will gain high visibility across the University and with international partners, influencing long-term research success.   What you will deliver:     Provide   strategic advice on Horizon Europe, translating evolving EU frameworks into practical institutional strategies and actions.   Design and lead capability-building initiatives that prepare researchers to engage confidently in Horizon Europe consortia.   Develop and manage a robust pipeline of Horizon Europe concepts, proposals and partnerships aligned with   University   strengths.   Coordinate complex pre-award activities, ensuring effective collaboration across faculties, central   teams   and external partners.   Identify   institutional readiness gaps and implement scalable tools,   guidance   and interventions to support compliant participation.       You may be a great fit   if   you :     Bring several years’ experience in research grants administration within complex, research-intensive organisations.   Demonstrate   expertise   supporting Horizon Europe Pillar II or similar large-scale multinational funding schemes from ideation to delivery.   Provide   high-level strategic advice, distilling ambiguous information, assessing risks and opportunities, and proposing workable solutions.   Build and sustain strong relationships with senior stakeholders, including international partners and cross-institutional project teams.   Lead complex cross-functional initiatives, coordinating diverse contributors to deliver strategic research funding outcomes on tight timelines.   Excel in written communication, producing clear guidance and briefings, while exercising autonomous judgement as a senior specialist.   For further   information   please refer to the attached P osition   D esc ription .    What we offer you!     We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!     Your new team –   Melbourne Research and Enterprise   You will join   the International Research Grants team in   Melbourne Research and Enterprise, driving Strategy 2030 and Advancing Research 2030 across the University. Based in the Office of Research Management, you will collaborate closely with grants, contracts,   ethics   and finance experts.   The team partners with Melbourne Enterprise and faculties to translate world-class discovery into impact through funding and partnerships. You will be surrounded by collaborative, forward-looking colleagues committed to excellence,   innovation   and global research engagement.   Together, you will position   the University   as a trusted research partner of choice in Europe and beyond.     Be Yourself     The   University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe   organisation . The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.   Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're   committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.   Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process .   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants     We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians.   Visit   our   Indigenous staff page   to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.            Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at   korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit   \"apply with us\"   to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   This role is eligible for visa   sponsorship   and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process.   A Working with Children Check is   required   for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    Please upload your responses to Selection Criteria 1, 2, 3, 5 and 9, which can be found in the Position Description.       Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact   Patricia Gigliuto :   plgi@unimellb.edu.au     For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply   today, and   join a community   that’s   shaping the future.   Applications close:   11   September   11 :55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:       JR-015137 Horizon EU Specialist -PD.pdf   JR-015137 Horizon EU Specialist -PD.docx"
    },
